<%# What the page's context and colouring mean, in a line or two. Locals: logo
    (optional; a brand page passes its own on a tab that shows text versions,
    so the line about them can be true), brand_logo (optional: a brand page's
    logo on every tab, for its colouring line). %>
<% logo = local_assigns[:logo] %>
<% brand_logo = local_assigns[:brand_logo] %>
<p class="text-2xs text-muted" data-test="transparent-note">Every logo file has a transparent background, and here every logo sits on the page's own background. Context switches the hub between its light and dark theme, and the logos follow.</p>
<p class="text-2xs text-muted" data-test="colouring-note">Colouring: Original is the brand's own art, as its kit draws it; Alt is one solid colour per part.<%= " Brands with no original show Alt." unless brand_logo %></p>
<% if brand_logo && !brand_logo.original? %>
  <p class="text-sm text-secondary" data-test="no-original"><%= Logos::Variant.brand_name(brand_logo) %>'s logo is solid colour, so it has no separate original.</p>
<% end %>
<% if @context == :watermark %>
  <p class="text-2xs text-muted" data-test="watermark-note">
    A watermark is the whole logo in one colour, slightly transparent, for placing over a photograph. Watermark turns the page dark to show it. Colouring does not apply to a watermark: it is always the single-colour version.
    <% if logo && logo.highlight == "colour" %>
      <span data-test="watermark-text-note">This brand leads with a colour, and a watermark has only one, so its three text versions look the same here.</span>
    <% end %>
  </p>
<% end %>
